From 7c7bb854dc4f4a8e0961e7211dba8d353b790522 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexey Makhalov <alexey.makhalov@broadcom.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 20:41:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] init/main.c: drain per-CPU page caches after boot-time memory
 frees

free_initmem()/mark_readonly() release several MB of memory back to
the allocator during boot (unused kernel image, decrypted memory,
initrd, text/rodata and rodata/data alignment gaps). These frees can
leave a large burst of pages sitting in the per-CPU pagevec (pcp)
caches instead of the zone's shared free list, because
NR_FREE_PAGES/MemFree is only updated when a page lands on the shared
free list, not merely when cached per-CPU.

mm/page_alloc.c's adaptive pcp->high heuristic grows the per-CPU cache
watermark in response to bursts of consecutive same-CPU frees with no
intervening allocation, and does not shrink back down until enough
allocation activity happens afterward (or memory pressure/compaction
forces a drain). On a canister-enabled build, whose larger/differently
structured __init section causes a bigger such burst than a
non-canister build, this was observed to strand ~7MB in the boot CPU's
pcp list:

    # before this patch (canister build)
    pagesets
      cpu: 0
        count: 1891
        high:  2310
    Mem: total=107 used=15 free=90 (MB)

    # after this patch
    pagesets
      cpu: 0
        count: 16
        high:  2310
    Mem: total=107 used=8 free=98 (MB)

This memory was never lost or leaked -- it is fully usable and gets
reclaimed into MemFree the moment normal allocation activity occurs --
but the delay makes any memory-overhead measurement taken shortly
after boot non-deterministic and dependent on exactly how the boot-time
free bursts interact with this heuristic. Explicitly drain all pcp
lists once the boot-time frees are done and before userspace starts, so
/proc/meminfo reflects real, settled numbers from the first read.

Signed-off-by: Alexey Makhalov <alexey.makhalov@broadcom.com>
---
 init/main.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/init/main.c b/init/main.c
index 73ebd2dcb..c17b4c770 100644
--- a/init/main.c
+++ b/init/main.c
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/initrd.h>
 #include <linux/memblock.h>
+#include <linux/gfp.h>
 #include <linux/acpi.h>
 #include <linux/bootconfig.h>
 #include <linux/console.h>
@@ -1455,6 +1456,17 @@ static int __ref kernel_init(void *unused)
 	free_initmem();
 	mark_readonly();
 
+	/*
+	 * The various free_*_mem() calls above can leave a large burst of
+	 * freed pages stranded in the per-CPU pagevec caches (pcp lists),
+	 * since NR_FREE_PAGES/MemFree is only updated when a page actually
+	 * lands on the zone's shared free list, not when it is cached
+	 * per-CPU. Drain explicitly here so boot-time memory accounting is
+	 * deterministic instead of depending on when the adaptive pcp->high
+	 * heuristic happens to shrink back down.
+	 */
+	drain_all_pages(NULL);
+
 	/*
 	 * Kernel mappings are now finalized - update the userspace page-table
 	 * to finalize PTI.
-- 
